WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.420
So for today, we will
be, first of all,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.080
wrapping up the discussion
on indirect methods.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.640
And then we'll be
introducing another class

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.320
of open loop control strategies,
which is one of direct methods.

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.080
And so specifically,
remember that last time

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:23.460
we basically were wrapping
up the discussion,

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.362
indirect methods, where
a bird's eye view,

00:00:26.362 --> 00:00:28.320
we were saying that
indirect methods represents

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.860
this class of solution methods
for optimal control problems,

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.600
whereby we define and derive
some optimality conditions which

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.800
we want to solve for generating
candidates for optimality.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.840
These optimal
conditions boiled down

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.200
into two-point boundary
value problems,

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.000
which we have to solve.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.880
And so, what we
did is essentially

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.080
started to look at
computational methods

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.580
for solving these two-point
boundary value problems,

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:58.480
whereby we first of all
introduced the solve BVP solver

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.790
in Python, specifically for
an abstract two-point boundary

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:03.650
value problem.

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.930
And so today we will be
using the same solve BVP,

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:10.070
but this time applied to an
actual optimal control problem.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:11.550
First of all, so
that you can see

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.770
how this can be
done in practice,

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.630
and then also for you
to have potentially

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:18.510
a useful template for
optimizing or for solving

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:22.790
other optimal control problems.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:27.430
But specifically, we were also
mentioning how these solve--

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:30.650
the solve BVP solvers, and most
solvers out there, to be honest,

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:32.630
for a two-point
boundary value problem,

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:36.930
assume that we specify the
problem in quote unquote,

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.870
"standard form,"
where, for example,

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:45.790
we were assuming that the
final time is given and known.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:47.712
And so we know
that in general, we

00:01:47.712 --> 00:01:49.670
have been dealing with
optimal control problems

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:51.150
where this is not the case.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.590
And so, there are
a number of tricks

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:57.180
that have been developed in
order to basically reformulate

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:00.180
any given structure of our
optimal control problem

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.725
into a form that is amenable
for classical solvers.

00:02:03.725 --> 00:02:05.100
And so, here there
is a reference

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:08.039
that I definitely
advise you to check out.

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:11.460
It's a fairly old reference,
dates back to the 1980s,

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.700
but it has all of these
collection of reformulation

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.780
tricks to indeed massage the
problem into one that falls

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.940
under this standard form.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:25.060
And specifically
for today, I wanted

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:29.220
to highlight one of the
most common settings whereby

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.900
the standard form
does not apply,

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.420
which is one of free final time.

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.900
So again, these solvers
usually assume that we

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:38.720
have a given final time.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.780
How do we reformulate a problem
where the final time is actually

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:44.260
a decision variable that
we want to pick in order

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.740
to be amenable for this?

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:52.140
And a usual trick that happens
oftentimes in mathematics

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:53.340
is the one of--

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.440
that is so-called
of time rescaling.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.560
Where specifically,
we will be introducing

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:16.200
where we model time, t, as a
normalized time, tau, times ts.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:21.160
Tau here is this rescaled
time which we wish to use.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:27.360
And note that by this
definition, tau is t over tf.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.520
And so, tf is something we
don't know, but whatever tf is,

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:33.740
tau will be defined
between 0 and 1.

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:35.240
Because at the final
time, this will

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:38.040
be tf over tf,
which is equal to 1.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.880
So overall, this
object is something

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:43.880
that is between 0 and 1.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:47.560
Now of course, when we do
this kind of reformulation,

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:52.000
we need to change
the time derivatives.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.590
And so specifically,
the time derivatives

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:58.750
over tau by the chain rule
will be the derivative

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:09.302
over time times the tau times
tf over the tau, which is indeed

00:04:09.302 --> 00:04:13.790
d under tau times tf.

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:17.070
So essentially, we want to
reformulate the problem in one

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:18.410
where the final time is known.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:20.390
We do this by rescaling
time according

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:23.610
to this new rescaled time
tau, which is between 0 and 1.

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:27.110
So in this rescaled time, final
time is known and equal to 1.

00:04:27.110 --> 00:04:29.110
When we do this
reformulation, we

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.990
need to adjust the time
derivatives, which essentially

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:37.150
entails multiplying our
time derivatives by tf.

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:39.510
The other step that is
entailed and restricted

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:43.230
is then to introduce
a dummy state variable

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:47.830
r, which corresponds
to the final time

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:52.140
and has trivial dynamics,
r dot equals to 0.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:55.220
So r replaces final
time, and in order

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:58.640
to model this in our two-point
boundary value problem,

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.000
we need to have some
associated dynamics with it.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.300
But we know that since r
replaces tf for us, which

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:07.580
is a constant, its
dynamics are trivial,

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:09.660
which essentially
boils down to meaning

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:13.660
that r is fixed over time.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:16.520
And so, this is basically all
that there is with this trick.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:20.300
So we do these modifications
and replace all instances of tf

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:22.520
with r in our formulation.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:26.520
So any questions about this?

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.060
But there will be
an example where

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:32.220
we go into the details of this.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.360
OK guys, pretty straightforward.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:37.740
And specifically, the
example, we will use

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:41.140
will relate to the
specific optimal control

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:44.540
problem we were solving
a couple of lectures back

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:47.340
where, if you remember,
we were trying to control

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:51.770
this particle on a line
system where we wanted to move

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:56.370
from position x 0 equal
to 10 with 0 velocity,

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:59.810
to a final position of the
origin, so x tf equal to 0,

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:01.190
also with 0 velocity.

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:03.890
And we wanted to optimize
this cost functional, which

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:06.010
was comprised of two terms--

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:08.890
one term weighting
the final time,

00:06:08.890 --> 00:06:13.090
and one term weighting the
control effort in our system.

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:16.910
And last week, we derived the
solution analytically for this,

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:21.590
but now let's model it using
the computational methods.

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:24.250
And specifically notice
that here, final time

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.290
is a free variable,
and so we will

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:29.290
have to implement exactly
these tricks and reformulations

00:06:29.290 --> 00:06:31.190
that we just described.

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:36.290
So essentially, what
we're doing here

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:40.250
is we're modeling this
particle on a line system,

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:42.870
where as usual for
our solved BVP,

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:46.450
we have to describe first of
all our dynamics, so the ODE.

00:06:50.037 --> 00:06:52.620
Actually, just to make sure that
we are getting this straight,

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:57.560
let me quickly
restate the solution,

00:06:57.560 --> 00:06:59.640
and the optimality
conditions which

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.002
we derived for this example.

00:07:01.002 --> 00:07:02.960
So this is-- I'll go
quite quickly through this

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:07.600
because this is what
we did last time, but.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:09.920
Essentially, we solved
this problem over here.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:11.540
And so, if you
remember to solve this,

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:13.900
we applied our
classical strategy.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:16.840
So we derived the
optimality conditions

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:21.240
which entails, first of all,
taking our system, which

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.040
in this case is a
second order system,

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:28.320
and write it as a
first order system

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:31.640
by augmenting with one
additional state variable.

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:34.280
So x double dot
equals u essentially

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:43.400
becomes x1 dot equals
x2, and x2 dot equals u.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.900
Then once we reformulate
it as a first order system,

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:49.570
we could apply nominally
our optimality conditions.

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:51.350
So the first thing
that we do, as usual,

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:54.190
is we formulate the
Hamiltonian, which

00:07:54.190 --> 00:08:02.350
for us is one half u squared
plus the costate variables

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:03.870
transpose our dynamics.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:19.190
So p1, p2 times x to u, which is
equal to 1/2 u squared plus p1

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:23.630
x2 plus p2 u.

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:25.210
So this is our Hamiltonian.

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:29.470
And once we have this, we can
apply our optimality conditions.

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:32.650
And as usual, let's
just write them down.

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:35.510
So the first
optimality condition

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:37.650
essentially represents
the dynamics.

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:42.270
So where we know that x dot is
equal to the partial derivative

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:45.770
of the Hamiltonian with
respect to the costates p,

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:51.330
and this is exactly
our dynamics over here.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:55.210
The second condition
entails setting

00:08:55.210 --> 00:08:59.130
key dot equal to minus
the partial derivative

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:03.490
of the Hamiltonian
with respect to x.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:06.050
Where by doing the partial
derivative of the Hamiltonian

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:09.050
with respect to our two
costate variables, what we get

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:19.270
is p1 dot equals 0, and
p2 dot equals minus p1.

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:27.090
So the first one is the
derivative of the Hamiltonian

00:09:27.090 --> 00:09:28.290
with respect to x1.

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:29.630
There is no x1 here.

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:33.790
The second one is with respect
to x2, so there is negative p1.

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:39.770
And then the third
optimality condition

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:43.320
entails setting
the-- so essentially,

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:45.920
imposing that u has to
be a stationary point

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:48.480
of our Hamiltonian,
which essentially means

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:51.640
that the derivative of the
Hamiltonian with respect to u

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:55.280
has to be equal to 0.

00:09:55.280 --> 00:10:03.600
Which in this case means that
bu plus p2 has to be equal to 0,

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:07.020
and so essentially,
u is minus p2 over b.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:15.440
And so, here we can update
this as minus P2 over B.

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:18.520
Because remember that usually,
mechanically how we solve this

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:20.880
is that we use this
third condition

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:23.120
to express u as a
function of x and p,

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:25.160
and then we do the
appropriate substitutions

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:27.720
in our differential equations.

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:30.840
So these are our conditions.

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:33.960
In order to solve these
differential equations, as

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:47.024
usual, we need boundary
conditions where

00:10:47.024 --> 00:10:54.065
we know the state at our
initial and terminal--

00:10:54.065 --> 00:10:55.690
we know the initial
and terminal state,

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:58.630
so we know that x1 at
time 0 is equal to 10,

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:02.910
we know that x2 at
time 0 is equal to 0.

00:11:02.910 --> 00:11:06.510
We know that x1 at tf
has to be equal to 0,

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:11.110
and we know that x2 at
tf has to be equal to 0.

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:13.510
So we have our four
boundary conditions.

00:11:13.510 --> 00:11:15.630
There we have five
constants of integration

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:18.350
that we will have to pinpoint.

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:20.230
And so the additional
boundary conditions

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:23.470
come from our master
boundary condition,

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:25.510
where this depends on
whether the final state

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:27.710
or the final times
are fixed or free.

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:29.470
And so in this case,
we are in a case

00:11:29.470 --> 00:11:31.710
where the final time is free.

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:36.030
And so, if you remember
from the past lectures,

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:38.820
this additional boundary
condition essentially

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:42.820
entails setting the
Hamiltonian evaluated

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:48.220
at the final time plus the
derivative of the terminal cost

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:53.655
in the t, also evaluated at the
final time, setting this to 0.

00:11:53.655 --> 00:11:55.780
Now, I won't go through
the derivation of the terms

00:11:55.780 --> 00:12:00.940
because we did this already, but
the result of this is the last

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:08.540
boundary condition, which boils
down to minus P2 squared tf over

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:14.200
2b plus alpha ts has
to be equal to 0.

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:19.540
All right, and so here
we have our system

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:23.600
of differential equations, and
here our boundary conditions.

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:32.820
Everything here
is not new, I just

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:35.460
re-derived it so that
we can then visually

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:40.530
compare what we are
implementing in the code.

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:42.930
And so, going back to
the code, essentially

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:45.730
what we have here is, first
we define our differential

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:47.330
equations.

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:53.290
So if you see x1 is equal--
x1 dot is equal to x2,

00:12:53.290 --> 00:12:57.850
we have x2 dot is equal
to negative p2 over p.

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:02.050
We then have that--

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:07.330
yes, so p1 dot is equal to 0 and
p2 dot is equal to negative p1.

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:10.810
And then here we already are
using this reformulation trick

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:13.050
which we just
derived, whereby we

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:16.890
are adding this additional
dummy state variable

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:18.990
r with trivial dynamics.

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:23.050
Specifically, r dot
has to be equal to 0.

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:25.850
And we are rescaling
these dynamics by tier--

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:30.790
by the time derivative, which
we just discussed before.

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:35.740
Now given this, we also have
the boundary conditions.

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:37.280
Specifically,
remember that these

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:40.600
are fed to the solver as
residuals, so we have x1

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:46.000
has to be equal to 10,
x2 has to be equal to 0,

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:48.880
x1 also has to be-- x1
at the final stage has

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:52.400
to be equal to 0, x2 at the
final time has to be equal to 0,

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:56.060
and then we have our last
master boundary condition here.

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:06.120
And then, so we have
all of our ingredients.

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:10.500
So we pass to resolve BVP,
the differential equations.

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:12.680
We pass the boundary
conditions, we

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:15.680
specify an array of times--
of discretized times--

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:18.920
which now goes from 0 to
1 because we rescale time

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:24.040
to be between 0 and 1,
together with an initial guess

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:26.440
for our solutions.

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:29.640
And if we run this,
what we get is

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:34.000
that the final time is equal
to 4.47, which if you recall,

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:37.740
is exactly what we were getting
by the analytical solution.

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:42.100
So if you remember, our
analytical solution was looking

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:48.900
something like 1,800 times b
over a to the power of 1 over 5,

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.900
and this is 4.47.

00:14:51.900 --> 00:15:00.460
So we do get the same solution,
and we can also inspect

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:03.180
the state variables x1 and x2.

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:08.300
So x1 indeed at time 0 starts
at 10, and at the final time

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:10.980
reaches 0.

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:15.260
And we see also our control
of how we get there.

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:17.340
So this is-- essentially,
we reformulated

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:20.700
this two-point boundary value
problem into its standard form,

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:23.000
and solved it through
numerical methods.

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:30.600
Any questions about this?

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:36.070
Pretty straightforward, right?

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:46.130
And so, this concludes our
discussion on indirect methods.

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:47.810
Again, at a high
level, we saw how

00:15:47.810 --> 00:15:51.130
this entails specifying
optimality conditions

00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:53.690
and then passing them
through a numerical solver

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:57.370
to solve the resulting two-point
boundary value problem.

00:15:57.370 --> 00:15:58.930
Indirect methods
are, historically,

00:15:58.930 --> 00:16:03.010
how optimal control and
the overall field started,

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:06.690
and nowadays, I would say
direct methods are definitely

00:16:06.690 --> 00:16:10.610
increasingly more popular
because of their ease of use.

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:13.390
That said, indirect methods
have their strengths.

00:16:13.390 --> 00:16:15.930
For example, they can
ultimately boil down

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:20.450
to having an analytical solution
for your control, which is

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:22.130
ultimately the gold standard.

00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:25.570
If I have an analytical
solution, then essentially,

00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:28.410
computing in real time
an optimal control

00:16:28.410 --> 00:16:32.080
entails a forward pass through
a function, which is great.

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:34.780
Even if we're not able
to solve analytically,

00:16:34.780 --> 00:16:37.400
we might be able to just
derive the control structure

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:39.120
of our control profile.

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:42.260
Remember, last time we were
looking at bang-bang control,

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:46.520
bang-off bang control, which
is great for interpretability

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:49.920
and just overall system design.

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:51.880
But again, indeed
the field of robotics

00:16:51.880 --> 00:16:54.540
is definitely moving
towards direct methods,

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:57.180
so let's talk a little
bit about those.

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:01.420
So usual roadmap.

00:17:01.420 --> 00:17:05.760
We are always in the open
loop part of our roadmap,

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:07.700
looking at direct methods.

00:17:10.180 --> 00:17:10.680
Yes?

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:13.599
[INAUDIBLE]

00:17:13.599 --> 00:17:14.339
It is, it is.

00:17:14.339 --> 00:17:17.420
So all the code is up, and you
are free to play around with it.

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:17.920
Yeah?

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:23.500
OK.

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:29.230
So direct methods, of course,
at a high level approach

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:31.910
to, and try to solve the
exact same optimal control

00:17:31.910 --> 00:17:35.670
problem where we have a
cost functional, which

00:17:35.670 --> 00:17:38.110
we wish to optimize.

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:42.070
We have some dynamics, we have
an initial state and a terminal

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:45.190
state, here expressed
as having to belong

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:50.510
to some manifold m of f,
defined as the 0 level

00:17:50.510 --> 00:17:53.510
sets of a function
f, and we have

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:56.150
some-- we might have
some control constraints.

00:17:56.150 --> 00:17:57.910
So here, for simplicity,
I am assuming

00:17:57.910 --> 00:17:59.570
that the terminal
cost is equal to 0,

00:17:59.570 --> 00:18:01.330
but it could be trivially added.

00:18:01.330 --> 00:18:05.830
And we're also assuming that the
initial time, t0, is equal to 0.

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:10.230
But this is mostly for
simplicity and for notation.

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:12.630
So until now, indeed we were
approaching this problem

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:15.510
through indirect methods
where we defined and derived

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:17.310
optimality conditions,
and then we

00:18:17.310 --> 00:18:21.390
solved this as we just saw
through numerical methods,

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:24.910
whereby we're essentially
solving these two-point boundary

00:18:24.910 --> 00:18:26.780
value problems.

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:29.060
And indirect
methods are referred

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:33.180
to as optimized and discretized
exactly because of this reason.

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:37.220
The optimality conditions
relate to the optimized part,

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:40.460
and the solving for those
optimality conditions

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:43.140
through numerical
solvers relates

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:46.340
with the discretized part,
because numerical solvers

00:18:46.340 --> 00:18:50.500
internally are
discretizing time anyways.

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:53.780
Direct methods are if
you want the reciprocal

00:18:53.780 --> 00:18:57.060
of indirect methods,
and in a way,

00:18:57.060 --> 00:18:59.260
I would say that direct
methods are probably

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:03.238
the more natural approach to
approaching optimal control

00:19:03.238 --> 00:19:05.780
problems, if we were to have
discussed this a couple of weeks

00:19:05.780 --> 00:19:06.500
back.

00:19:06.500 --> 00:19:09.300
Because normally what we do
is we take the optimal control

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:13.060
problem in continuous
time, we discretize time,

00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:16.540
and turn this into a nonlinear
and constrained optimization

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:17.260
problem.

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:20.460
And then we can just throw
this optimization problem

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:28.290
to nonlinear solvers, and hope
to get a solution out of it.

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:31.270
And the reason why direct
methods are starting to become,

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:33.290
and are increasingly
more used in robotics

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:36.650
is because there have
been a lot of improvements

00:19:36.650 --> 00:19:39.130
in the field of
nonlinear optimization

00:19:39.130 --> 00:19:42.170
that is making the solution
of this problem feasible also

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:45.570
in real time.

00:19:45.570 --> 00:19:47.670
There are a number of
excellent references here.

00:19:47.670 --> 00:19:51.410
I'm just listing
a couple for you

00:19:51.410 --> 00:19:54.970
to look at if you're interested.

00:19:54.970 --> 00:19:59.050
But so, transcription of
direct methods at a high level

00:19:59.050 --> 00:20:06.770
also can be subdivided between
two subcategories of methods.

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:10.810
And the way these two families
differentiate from each other

00:20:10.810 --> 00:20:14.210
is based on how we
choose to transcribe--

00:20:14.210 --> 00:20:18.770
that is, discretized and remodel
as a discrete optimization

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:22.350
problem, the original
optimal control problem.

00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:25.470
And so we have namely state
and control parameterization

00:20:25.470 --> 00:20:29.310
methods, also known as
collocation methods, and control

00:20:29.310 --> 00:20:32.910
parameterization methods,
also known as shooting.

00:20:32.910 --> 00:20:35.650
So at a high level,
starting from the latter--

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:38.030
so the control
parameterization methods is

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:42.270
that we take the optimal control
problem in continuous time,

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:45.310
and we will be discretizing
time and assume

00:20:45.310 --> 00:20:49.670
that only the controls
for us are variables

00:20:49.670 --> 00:20:51.510
prone to optimization.

00:20:51.510 --> 00:20:55.450
So from the perspective of
the optimizer and the solver,

00:20:55.450 --> 00:20:58.190
only the control variables
will be variables

00:20:58.190 --> 00:21:00.030
we are optimizing over.

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:03.030
The states are
computed recursively

00:21:03.030 --> 00:21:07.310
through the unrolling
of the dynamics.

00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:09.950
On the other hand, state
and control parameterization

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:13.790
methods-- again, taking the
perspective of the black box

00:21:13.790 --> 00:21:15.630
non-linear solver--

00:21:15.630 --> 00:21:20.860
will be transcriptions whereby
both the states and the controls

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:24.220
will be numerical optimization
variables we are free to tune,

00:21:24.220 --> 00:21:26.580
and the solver will
be free to tune.

00:21:26.580 --> 00:21:29.940
The difference is that in this
case-- so the state and control

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:32.660
parameterization
methods dynamics

00:21:32.660 --> 00:21:35.780
become constraints
that link together

00:21:35.780 --> 00:21:39.620
the control optimization
variables and the state

00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:41.300
optimization variables.

00:21:41.300 --> 00:21:43.240
Here, this is just a
high level picture,

00:21:43.240 --> 00:21:48.020
but we will see in detail
both of these classes today.

00:21:48.020 --> 00:21:51.860
Of course, these two
families have pros and cons.

00:21:51.860 --> 00:21:53.700
So as you can
probably imagine, we

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:58.180
are discussing these control
parameterization methods

00:21:58.180 --> 00:22:01.420
as methods whereby only the
control variables become

00:22:01.420 --> 00:22:02.780
optimization variables.

00:22:02.780 --> 00:22:07.420
And so, these tend to become
smaller optimization problems,

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:11.820
simply because we have less
variables to optimize over.

00:22:11.820 --> 00:22:22.210
At the same time, in this family
here, because we are enforcing--

00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:24.650
so the states are only
recursively computed

00:22:24.650 --> 00:22:27.970
through the unrolling and
propagation of our dynamics.

00:22:27.970 --> 00:22:30.890
Enforcing constraints
over states

00:22:30.890 --> 00:22:35.610
is definitely more challenging.

00:22:35.610 --> 00:22:38.050
Because if you imagine
the other approach

00:22:38.050 --> 00:22:39.610
where both states
and controls are

00:22:39.610 --> 00:22:42.090
optimization-based
variables, we can directly

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:46.130
impose state constraints by just
introducing a new constraint

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:47.590
on an optimization variable.

00:22:47.590 --> 00:22:49.470
We will see this more
in the next slide.

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:51.770
But here, just to give you
a feel of the pros and cons

00:22:51.770 --> 00:22:54.170
of different methods.

00:22:54.170 --> 00:22:57.050
So simply put, if--

00:22:57.050 --> 00:23:01.090
I would say, if you have
complex state constraints

00:23:01.090 --> 00:23:05.410
that you would like to include
in your optimal control problem,

00:23:05.410 --> 00:23:07.730
then control
parameterization methods

00:23:07.730 --> 00:23:10.070
might be a good approach
because they're just simple,

00:23:10.070 --> 00:23:14.650
and you have less optimization
variables for your optimizers.

00:23:14.650 --> 00:23:17.260
On the other hand, if you
have complex state constraints

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:18.760
that you would like
to enforce, then

00:23:18.760 --> 00:23:21.800
probably state and control
parameterization methods

00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:22.860
are the way to go.

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:26.560
Yes?

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:30.720
Do you ever do both in parallel
as a way to approximate?

00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:33.120
It's a great question,
because there are definitely

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:37.720
hybrid formulations known as
multiple shooting methods.

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:40.740
I will discuss them in a few--
we won't cover them in depth,

00:23:40.740 --> 00:23:42.820
but I'll definitely give
you pointers for those.

00:23:42.820 --> 00:23:44.760
But yes, so you're
hitting on a point

00:23:44.760 --> 00:23:47.200
where these pros and cons
can be mixed and matched

00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:48.640
through hybrid methods.

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:58.200
OK, so let's start from
the state and control

00:23:58.200 --> 00:23:59.500
parameterization method.

00:23:59.500 --> 00:24:02.600
So at a high level,
what we want to do

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:06.120
is take our continuous time
optimal control problem

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:10.120
and turn it into a discrete
time nonlinear optimal control

00:24:10.120 --> 00:24:12.200
problem.

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:15.782
Where the way we
do this is we do--

00:24:15.782 --> 00:24:17.990
I mean, there are many
transcription methods and ways

00:24:17.990 --> 00:24:21.270
of discretizing time
that we could select,

00:24:21.270 --> 00:24:25.190
but here we tend
to use the simpler

00:24:25.190 --> 00:24:29.830
of these approaches, which
is Euler time discretization.

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:32.350
So in order to do this
step from continuous time

00:24:32.350 --> 00:24:35.190
to discrete time, what we
do is that we select a time

00:24:35.190 --> 00:24:38.590
discretization, meaning
we discretize time

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:44.290
from t0 to tn according to some
time interval which we pick.

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:49.630
And then for all
of our time steps--

00:24:49.630 --> 00:24:55.070
so t0 through tn, which now are
just indices-- we assume what

00:24:55.070 --> 00:24:59.190
is called a 0 order
hold on our control,

00:24:59.190 --> 00:25:07.390
namely that the control between
ti and ti plus 1 is equal to ui.

00:25:07.390 --> 00:25:11.590
So we assume that the control is
fixed in between a single time

00:25:11.590 --> 00:25:12.370
interval.

00:25:19.940 --> 00:25:22.220
So the other thing
that we need to do

00:25:22.220 --> 00:25:25.820
is essentially, we
use Euler integration

00:25:25.820 --> 00:25:28.040
to integrate the dynamics.

00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:31.480
[WHITEBOARD SQUEAKING]

00:25:36.380 --> 00:25:37.760
And remember that.

00:25:37.760 --> 00:25:47.100
So the dynamics, x dot of t
equals f, x, u, t, and now

00:25:47.100 --> 00:25:55.540
we can approximate this as x
of t plus delta t minus x of t

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:57.480
over delta t.

00:26:02.260 --> 00:26:04.940
And so from this, what
we can essentially

00:26:04.940 --> 00:26:17.480
write is that xt plus delta t is
equal to xt plus delta t times f

00:26:17.480 --> 00:26:19.040
of x, u, t.

00:26:22.080 --> 00:26:25.800
And in this slide,
I'm calling delta th.

00:26:33.980 --> 00:26:34.480
Yes?

00:26:34.480 --> 00:26:37.500
[INAUDIBLE]

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:39.720
Definitely, yeah.

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:42.520
Here we're just going for
the simplest approach,

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:45.300
but you could think of
more sophisticated ones.

00:26:48.400 --> 00:26:49.920
So at the high
level again, we want

00:26:49.920 --> 00:26:52.000
to go from continuous
time formulation

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:54.280
to the discrete
time formulation.

00:26:54.280 --> 00:26:56.560
We do this by
discretizing time, so

00:26:56.560 --> 00:27:00.680
selecting a range of indices
that represent our time.

00:27:00.680 --> 00:27:05.000
We assume a 0 order
hold on our controls,

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:10.030
and approximate our continuous
time dynamics in this way.

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:12.230
And then the last thing
that we have to do

00:27:12.230 --> 00:27:15.550
is also discretize the
cost, which is here

00:27:15.550 --> 00:27:18.230
represented by this integral.

00:27:18.230 --> 00:27:24.310
But notice that if we consider
the integral between ti and ti

00:27:24.310 --> 00:27:34.150
plus 1 of gx ut into
t, once we are assuming

00:27:34.150 --> 00:27:38.510
that we have this 0 order
hold on our controls,

00:27:38.510 --> 00:27:42.390
then the integral between--
so the cost between ti

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:47.190
and ti plus 1, this is
essentially a constant.

00:27:47.190 --> 00:27:56.822
And so, we can approximate
this as g of x ut times h,

00:27:56.822 --> 00:28:00.270
because we can take g
outside of the integral.

00:28:00.270 --> 00:28:03.710
And so we know that
we just call this

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:07.480
basically the integral between
a single time step as h.

00:28:13.660 --> 00:28:15.440
And that's done, basically.

00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:21.340
So then you could say, why even
bother spending three lectures

00:28:21.340 --> 00:28:22.900
on indirect methods
if this is all

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:26.220
you have to do to solve
optimal control problems?

00:28:26.220 --> 00:28:31.180
Again, we said indirect
methods have their benefits,

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:37.020
I would say especially now in
the age of LLMs, to be honest.

00:28:37.020 --> 00:28:38.540
Yeah, I'm sure we
all know that LLMs

00:28:38.540 --> 00:28:43.780
can do a great job at solving
these PMP problems aside

00:28:43.780 --> 00:28:46.260
from our homeworks, of course.

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:48.800
But the point is that
I think through LLMs,

00:28:48.800 --> 00:28:50.900
there is also this notion
where we can definitely

00:28:50.900 --> 00:28:53.380
accelerate the design
and development

00:28:53.380 --> 00:28:58.140
process in characterizing the
optimality conditions, which

00:28:58.140 --> 00:29:00.340
is arguably one of the
cases why people are going

00:29:00.340 --> 00:29:01.940
for these indirect methods--

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:03.600
direct methods in
the first place,

00:29:03.600 --> 00:29:05.770
because it's just simpler.

00:29:05.770 --> 00:29:08.250
Anyways, this is a parenthesis.

00:29:08.250 --> 00:29:09.930
But so, this is
basically all that there

00:29:09.930 --> 00:29:14.770
is to it on the state and
control parameterization method.

00:29:14.770 --> 00:29:16.110
Any questions on this?

00:29:16.110 --> 00:29:18.730
We'll see an example
shortly on how this

00:29:18.730 --> 00:29:20.410
is implemented in practice.

00:29:20.410 --> 00:29:23.990
But any questions on at a
high level what we're doing?

00:29:30.370 --> 00:29:33.730
So indeed, let's see
this in practice.

00:29:33.730 --> 00:29:41.210
So let's solve this problem
of crossing the river, known

00:29:41.210 --> 00:29:43.050
as Zermelo's Problem.

00:29:43.050 --> 00:29:46.050
You have a version of
this also in your pset.

00:29:46.050 --> 00:29:47.930
And specifically,
the setting here

00:29:47.930 --> 00:29:51.370
is that we want to cross
from an origin point

00:29:51.370 --> 00:29:55.890
to another destination point
on the other side of the river.

00:29:55.890 --> 00:30:01.250
The river has some flow, which
is 0 at the two extremes,

00:30:01.250 --> 00:30:03.880
and stronger in the middle.

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:06.760
And specifically
here we are assuming

00:30:06.760 --> 00:30:09.080
we have a constant
forward velocity, v,

00:30:09.080 --> 00:30:12.040
and the control for
us is represented

00:30:12.040 --> 00:30:19.520
by the angle between the
forward velocity and the flow.

00:30:19.520 --> 00:30:23.800
And specifically, we
want to reach this task

00:30:23.800 --> 00:30:27.200
by minimizing the
control effort, expressed

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:30.960
as the squared of our
control variable u.

00:30:30.960 --> 00:30:34.640
Why does the angle represent
a notion of control effort?

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:38.880
It's because if you imagine
taking the 180 degrees

00:30:38.880 --> 00:30:40.437
angle with respect
to the flow, this

00:30:40.437 --> 00:30:42.520
is when you're doing maximum
effort because you're

00:30:42.520 --> 00:30:44.760
essentially going
exactly against the flow.

00:30:44.760 --> 00:30:48.480
If it's 0, you're following the
flow and then you have no cost.

00:30:48.480 --> 00:30:50.640
So this problem
essentially models the fact

00:30:50.640 --> 00:30:52.680
where we want to
reach our destination

00:30:52.680 --> 00:30:55.160
point on the other
side of the river

00:30:55.160 --> 00:30:57.620
while minimizing
overall control effort.

00:31:04.269 --> 00:31:13.790
Are there any bounds [INAUDIBLE]
force of your [INAUDIBLE]?

00:31:13.790 --> 00:31:17.150
For example, can you
actually go straight

00:31:17.150 --> 00:31:19.650
backwards towards the
flow, [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:22.670 --> 00:31:25.110
I guess your-- [INAUDIBLE]--

00:31:25.110 --> 00:31:27.010
acceleration bounds
are like, how far.

00:31:27.010 --> 00:31:27.510
Yeah.

00:31:27.510 --> 00:31:29.450
So here we don't assume
we have acceleration,

00:31:29.450 --> 00:31:31.910
meaning we assume we have
a constant velocity, v,

00:31:31.910 --> 00:31:34.270
and we are controlling
this angle.

00:31:34.270 --> 00:31:37.690
And we assume our
angle has some bounds.

00:31:37.690 --> 00:31:40.910
So we have some umax.

00:31:40.910 --> 00:31:42.610
Does this answer your question?

00:31:47.870 --> 00:31:52.490
So here we are indeed
defining our problem.

00:31:52.490 --> 00:31:55.070
So we assume that tf is 10.

00:31:55.070 --> 00:32:01.210
We have our final
coordinates, 10 and 5.

00:32:01.210 --> 00:32:04.250
Here we represent the flow
through this lambda function,

00:32:04.250 --> 00:32:12.930
where ultimately if
y is equal to l-- so

00:32:12.930 --> 00:32:16.990
we are at the upper
extreme of our river.

00:32:16.990 --> 00:32:18.010
This is equal to 0.

00:32:21.010 --> 00:32:23.590
If y is equal to
0, this is also 0.

00:32:23.590 --> 00:32:26.850
So this basically encodes that
at the two borders of the river

00:32:26.850 --> 00:32:29.790
the flow is 0, and it
is maximum in between.

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:37.730
So here we are representing
our optimization variables

00:32:37.730 --> 00:32:41.170
through basically a
concatenation in a vector.

00:32:41.170 --> 00:32:47.010
So our z variable represents
all of our x, y and u's, where

00:32:47.010 --> 00:32:50.390
xy is the position that
we have on the plane.

00:32:53.450 --> 00:32:58.560
So this is our river,
this would be x,

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:02.760
and this will be y,
where this will be--

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:07.540
this point will be L,
and this will be m.

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:19.160
And so, first of
all, we introduce

00:33:19.160 --> 00:33:22.380
and we define our bounds
for our variables.

00:33:22.380 --> 00:33:26.480
Specifically, the x's have
to be between 0 and m,

00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:31.120
and our y's have to
be between 0 and l.

00:33:31.120 --> 00:33:33.480
And for our controls,
we are bounded

00:33:33.480 --> 00:33:36.040
between negative umax and umax.

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:42.680
We define our cost
function as the sum

00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:47.240
of the squares of
our u variables

00:33:47.240 --> 00:33:51.920
multiplied by h according
to our discretization.

00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:54.160
And then we define
our constraints.

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:58.670
Specifically, our
dynamics on the x variable

00:33:58.670 --> 00:34:03.190
such that the
states xi plus 1 are

00:34:03.190 --> 00:34:09.469
equal to xi plus h
times our dynamics.

00:34:09.469 --> 00:34:10.610
Same thing for the y.

00:34:10.610 --> 00:34:18.389
So yi plus 1 is equal at
yi plus our flow dynamics.

00:34:18.389 --> 00:34:21.330
And then we have the initial
and terminal constraints.

00:34:21.330 --> 00:34:27.070
So we basically say that the
first variables of both x and y

00:34:27.070 --> 00:34:33.909
have to be equal to 0
and m or l respectively.

00:34:33.909 --> 00:34:37.949
And so-- is this clear
how we are basically

00:34:37.949 --> 00:34:40.570
mapping from the actual
formulation to this code?

00:34:40.570 --> 00:34:41.830
[INAUDIBLE]

00:34:41.830 --> 00:34:44.650
Yeah, so MRL is this
point over here.

00:34:44.650 --> 00:34:50.989
So this would be our coordinate
ml, and we start from 0 0.

00:34:50.989 --> 00:34:54.260
So is this your final
location, where m is--

00:34:54.260 --> 00:34:55.639
Is the x the--

00:34:55.639 --> 00:34:57.900
[INAUDIBLE]

00:34:57.900 --> 00:35:00.660
Yeah.

00:35:00.660 --> 00:35:01.460
Yes?

00:35:01.460 --> 00:35:04.380
So is your state here z?

00:35:04.380 --> 00:35:10.660
In this code, we are basically
defining z as the concatenation

00:35:10.660 --> 00:35:14.218
of x, y and u.

00:35:14.218 --> 00:35:16.260
So I guess my question is
like, why are including

00:35:16.260 --> 00:35:18.500
the control in the first place?

00:35:18.500 --> 00:35:23.060
This is just to
handle it in code.

00:35:23.060 --> 00:35:23.920
That makes sense?

00:35:27.220 --> 00:35:29.580
Yes?

00:35:29.580 --> 00:35:32.860
In this model we are
completely noiseless.

00:35:32.860 --> 00:35:35.740
[INAUDIBLE] perfectly
described, our motion dynamics

00:35:35.740 --> 00:35:36.800
are perfectly described.

00:35:36.800 --> 00:35:37.300
Yes.

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:43.660
If we think about
the [INAUDIBLE] would

00:35:43.660 --> 00:35:45.700
you think of solving
it the same way,

00:35:45.700 --> 00:35:48.620
but then solving it 10
times, or something?

00:35:48.620 --> 00:35:50.580
[INAUDIBLE]

00:35:50.580 --> 00:35:51.800
It's a great question.

00:35:51.800 --> 00:35:54.490
So here we are assuming
the full open loop

00:35:54.490 --> 00:35:58.250
strategy, where we assume the
dynamics are perfectly known.

00:35:58.250 --> 00:35:59.810
Of course, the
moment-- in reality,

00:35:59.810 --> 00:36:03.250
dynamics are never perfectly
known, so usually what happens

00:36:03.250 --> 00:36:05.650
is that your control
stack will definitely

00:36:05.650 --> 00:36:07.570
have a hierarchy of modules.

00:36:07.570 --> 00:36:09.950
Maybe you would have
some open loop planner,

00:36:09.950 --> 00:36:13.810
which basically
produces a rough plan

00:36:13.810 --> 00:36:15.170
that I would like to follow.

00:36:15.170 --> 00:36:17.430
But then in real time, when
I'm actually deploying,

00:36:17.430 --> 00:36:20.210
I will probably have a
closed loop module, which

00:36:20.210 --> 00:36:21.890
basically tracks this plan.

00:36:21.890 --> 00:36:25.970
This closed loop model will
then be tasked with the problem

00:36:25.970 --> 00:36:30.630
of, say, that this is
the path that I decide,

00:36:30.630 --> 00:36:33.550
but in reality, I will
be bounced off this path.

00:36:33.550 --> 00:36:37.110
The closed loop will tend me to
steer towards my nominal path.

00:36:37.110 --> 00:36:40.270
So all of this logic is
performed via the program?

00:36:40.270 --> 00:36:40.770
Yes.

00:36:40.770 --> 00:36:42.170
So this is one way.

00:36:42.170 --> 00:36:44.190
One way is handling it
through closed loop.

00:36:44.190 --> 00:36:46.810
And I would say that in
general, any deployment

00:36:46.810 --> 00:36:49.850
has a closed loop flavor to it.

00:36:49.850 --> 00:36:52.680
Of course, you could also do
a more sophisticated open loop

00:36:52.680 --> 00:36:56.940
planning, where maybe I have
some notion of the uncertainty,

00:36:56.940 --> 00:37:00.680
which I can capture through some
random noise, random variable w,

00:37:00.680 --> 00:37:02.723
which might be,
maybe assuming it's

00:37:02.723 --> 00:37:04.640
Gaussian distributed or
something of the like,

00:37:04.640 --> 00:37:07.920
and then come up with more
sophisticated planning

00:37:07.920 --> 00:37:08.740
strategies.

00:37:13.400 --> 00:37:15.740
And so, we have all
of our ingredients.

00:37:15.740 --> 00:37:20.040
We need an initial guess,
and so here, we just come up

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:22.700
with a pretty nonsensical,
I would say, initial guess.

00:37:22.700 --> 00:37:28.080
So our x's and y's are
set to 1 for all the time,

00:37:28.080 --> 00:37:33.842
nru is basically 0.5 of
the max that we can solve.

00:37:33.842 --> 00:37:36.300
It doesn't have to be feasible,
it's just an initial guess.

00:37:42.720 --> 00:37:47.220
If we run this, what we get
is this solution over here.

00:37:47.220 --> 00:37:55.400
So you can see that indeed
we go from a time 0 to 10 5,

00:37:55.400 --> 00:37:59.200
which is how we define ml
following this trajectory,

00:37:59.200 --> 00:38:01.820
and this is a visualization
of the optimal control

00:38:01.820 --> 00:38:03.080
profile over time.

00:38:05.740 --> 00:38:11.060
Now note that in this case, we
were essentially assuming that

00:38:11.060 --> 00:38:16.725
our umax-- so our bounds of
our controls-- was equal to 1.

00:38:16.725 --> 00:38:18.100
And if you look
a little bit more

00:38:18.100 --> 00:38:21.100
closely at our control profile,
we never really reached

00:38:21.100 --> 00:38:22.000
the boundary.

00:38:22.000 --> 00:38:25.940
So this boundary, our control,
is basically non-enforcing,

00:38:25.940 --> 00:38:27.300
or non-restrictive.

00:38:27.300 --> 00:38:32.380
So let's try to
rerun this, but now

00:38:32.380 --> 00:38:36.500
taking a slightly more
restrictive umax, which is 0.75.

00:38:36.500 --> 00:38:40.020
Which, if you notice, slightly
below our current optimal

00:38:40.020 --> 00:38:41.260
control profile.

00:38:41.260 --> 00:38:43.740
[INAUDIBLE]

00:38:45.240 --> 00:38:45.740
Yes.

00:38:45.740 --> 00:38:49.390
So u for us is the angle, and
so we are indeed assuming.

00:38:53.168 --> 00:38:54.710
So if we just run
this, first of all,

00:38:54.710 --> 00:38:56.030
it's taking a little
bit more time,

00:38:56.030 --> 00:38:57.155
which is never a good sign.

00:38:57.155 --> 00:39:00.430
And then we see that it
basically produces garbage.

00:39:00.430 --> 00:39:05.170
So we are not even starting
from our initial point,

00:39:05.170 --> 00:39:07.490
we're not reaching
our final point.

00:39:07.490 --> 00:39:11.170
Basically, lost in the river.

00:39:11.170 --> 00:39:14.550
And this is also what
the optimizer says,

00:39:14.550 --> 00:39:17.690
so we don't reach
a final solution.

00:39:17.690 --> 00:39:21.290
And this is obviously
just a specific case,

00:39:21.290 --> 00:39:24.570
but I want to use this
as an example of the fact

00:39:24.570 --> 00:39:29.270
that in moving from
indirect to direct methods,

00:39:29.270 --> 00:39:33.170
we are basically transitioning
a lot of the effort

00:39:33.170 --> 00:39:35.370
from the formulation
of the problem

00:39:35.370 --> 00:39:38.490
and the optimality
conditions to tweaking

00:39:38.490 --> 00:39:41.450
and making sure that the
optimization actually

00:39:41.450 --> 00:39:43.050
runs successfully.

00:39:43.050 --> 00:39:45.160
And so, this is why
direct methods are also

00:39:45.160 --> 00:39:50.320
much more susceptible to the
quality of the initial guess,

00:39:50.320 --> 00:39:54.480
and all of many other
properties that enable

00:39:54.480 --> 00:39:57.752
it to be well-conditioned.

00:39:57.752 --> 00:39:59.960
This is because if you think
about it from the solver

00:39:59.960 --> 00:40:03.400
perspective, the
solver is basically--

00:40:03.400 --> 00:40:05.880
it doesn't know we're solving
an optimal control problem.

00:40:05.880 --> 00:40:10.000
It just sees a random
nonlinear optimization problem,

00:40:10.000 --> 00:40:13.960
and it's trying to do its best.

00:40:13.960 --> 00:40:15.640
So for example, a
classical strategy

00:40:15.640 --> 00:40:19.200
that people use to generate
better initial guesses

00:40:19.200 --> 00:40:25.440
is to first solve a simplified
version of the full problem

00:40:25.440 --> 00:40:26.640
that we wish to solve.

00:40:26.640 --> 00:40:29.400
For example, we might be
relaxing some constraints

00:40:29.400 --> 00:40:32.000
or loosening some
of the constraints,

00:40:32.000 --> 00:40:34.720
and then using this
intermediate solution

00:40:34.720 --> 00:40:39.280
as a candidate initial
guess for further iteration

00:40:39.280 --> 00:40:41.280
of our problem.

00:40:41.280 --> 00:40:43.140
And so, let's do just that.

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:46.190
So here, what we're
doing is that we first

00:40:46.190 --> 00:40:50.310
run the same optimization
with the umax equal 1--

00:40:50.310 --> 00:40:52.190
so the one where
we were actually

00:40:52.190 --> 00:40:53.730
able to solve the problem.

00:40:53.730 --> 00:40:57.950
And then, let's rerun
the 0.75 version where

00:40:57.950 --> 00:40:59.670
we are assuming
now-- we are using

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:05.110
the previous solve as an initial
guess for the second solve.

00:41:05.110 --> 00:41:09.830
And what we get is that now this
converges, and it does indeed

00:41:09.830 --> 00:41:12.830
follow the bounds
on the controls.

00:41:12.830 --> 00:41:15.030
Again, many of the discussions
here are definitely

00:41:15.030 --> 00:41:16.550
problem specific,
but this is just

00:41:16.550 --> 00:41:19.190
a hint of the kind of
sophistications that have

00:41:19.190 --> 00:41:21.290
to come with direct methods.

00:41:26.440 --> 00:41:28.650
OK, any-- yes?

00:41:28.650 --> 00:41:32.430
If your relaxed
version gives you

00:41:32.430 --> 00:41:36.030
a control input that's higher
than 0.75 at the beginning,

00:41:36.030 --> 00:41:39.230
or some state where
you're outside of bounds

00:41:39.230 --> 00:41:45.038
at the beginning, how would that
play out in your [INAUDIBLE]?

00:41:45.038 --> 00:41:46.080
Actually, think about it.

00:41:46.080 --> 00:41:47.460
This is what was happening here.

00:41:47.460 --> 00:41:50.660
Because the solution
with the bounds to one

00:41:50.660 --> 00:41:56.500
was actually breaking
the 0.75 bounds.

00:41:56.500 --> 00:41:58.260
But the point is
that initial guesses

00:41:58.260 --> 00:42:00.520
don't have to be feasible.

00:42:00.520 --> 00:42:04.260
So it just has to
be an initial point

00:42:04.260 --> 00:42:06.260
in solution space
for the optimizer

00:42:06.260 --> 00:42:10.540
to start iterating from.

00:42:10.540 --> 00:42:11.700
Does that make sense?

00:42:11.700 --> 00:42:12.500
Like the time 0.

00:42:12.500 --> 00:42:15.073
If it's at time 0--

00:42:15.073 --> 00:42:15.740
or actually, no.

00:42:15.740 --> 00:42:17.823
Never mind, I
think I understand.

00:42:17.823 --> 00:42:20.240
In general, the initial guess
doesn't have to be feasible.

00:42:20.240 --> 00:42:24.723
So it doesn't really mind
if there's no point in--

00:42:24.723 --> 00:42:27.140
it doesn't really affect the
optimization if it's feasible

00:42:27.140 --> 00:42:28.140
or not.

00:42:28.140 --> 00:42:30.340
Of course, if it's
feasible, probably it's

00:42:30.340 --> 00:42:32.560
also a better initial guess.

00:42:36.340 --> 00:42:36.840
Yes?

00:42:36.840 --> 00:42:39.550
[INAUDIBLE]

00:42:40.050 --> 00:42:42.510
--that's just angle,
that's just in radians.

00:42:42.510 --> 00:42:47.290
But what would the unit
be on the control effort?

00:42:47.290 --> 00:42:50.330
If we're assuming it's
radians, and then we are--

00:42:50.330 --> 00:42:54.010
the cost for us is just the
square of the radians over time,

00:42:54.010 --> 00:42:54.750
essentially.

00:43:06.010 --> 00:43:10.970
Yes, so this is basically the
transcription for this example

00:43:10.970 --> 00:43:16.490
Zermelo Problem, where we
essentially discretize time.

00:43:16.490 --> 00:43:20.210
This led to discretized
dynamics where

00:43:20.210 --> 00:43:23.450
we're multiplying the
original dynamics with respect

00:43:23.450 --> 00:43:28.370
to our time discretization h,
and the integrals become sums.

00:43:28.370 --> 00:43:30.090
Now of course,
here, remember we're

00:43:30.090 --> 00:43:33.770
looking at these two broad
subcategories of direct methods,

00:43:33.770 --> 00:43:36.890
specifically the states and
control parameterization

00:43:36.890 --> 00:43:38.110
methods.

00:43:38.110 --> 00:43:41.470
And so, notice that here,
the optimization variables

00:43:41.470 --> 00:43:44.830
for our optimization
problems are both the states

00:43:44.830 --> 00:43:46.710
and the controls.

00:43:46.710 --> 00:43:49.630
Where these two
are tied together

00:43:49.630 --> 00:43:52.670
through the dynamics, which
essentially become constraints

00:43:52.670 --> 00:43:55.210
that tie controls
and states together.

00:44:00.350 --> 00:44:04.590
And these are the
results that we just saw.

00:44:04.590 --> 00:44:07.950
And so, moving to the other
family of methods, which

00:44:07.950 --> 00:44:11.710
is the control
parametrization methods,

00:44:11.710 --> 00:44:16.350
I would say that most of the
intuition directly applies.

00:44:16.350 --> 00:44:19.630
The only two differences are--

00:44:19.630 --> 00:44:23.570
specifically relate with
the optimization variables.

00:44:23.570 --> 00:44:27.030
So now in the control
parameterization methods, only

00:44:27.030 --> 00:44:32.070
the controls ui's will be our
variables for optimization,

00:44:32.070 --> 00:44:36.020
and the dynamics essentially
become the mechanism

00:44:36.020 --> 00:44:39.100
through which we are
implicitly encoding

00:44:39.100 --> 00:44:42.220
our states by
recursively computing

00:44:42.220 --> 00:44:44.100
the dynamics over time.

00:44:44.100 --> 00:44:49.940
So how we will see now in a
moment on the same example

00:44:49.940 --> 00:44:53.020
in code, we will see how
this essentially boils down

00:44:53.020 --> 00:44:57.300
to defining an optimization
problem over use, whereby

00:44:57.300 --> 00:45:00.700
internally we have this inner
loop where we are propagating

00:45:00.700 --> 00:45:02.580
forward our dynamics.

00:45:02.580 --> 00:45:06.680
And this is also why it's
referred to as shooting methods,

00:45:06.680 --> 00:45:11.100
because you could imagine we
are pictorially doing something

00:45:11.100 --> 00:45:13.540
where we have an
initial state x0,

00:45:13.540 --> 00:45:20.220
and then the optimization will
choose a set of u's-- so from u1

00:45:20.220 --> 00:45:23.060
to un--

00:45:23.060 --> 00:45:25.900
and then internally, we will
have this recursive application

00:45:25.900 --> 00:45:27.980
of our dynamics
which essentially

00:45:27.980 --> 00:45:31.860
propagates forwards our states.

00:45:31.860 --> 00:45:37.030
But apart from that,
everything else stays the same.

00:45:37.030 --> 00:45:40.130
We are again assuming a
Euler discretization of time.

00:45:40.130 --> 00:45:43.290
We use Euler integration
to basically solve

00:45:43.290 --> 00:45:46.930
integrate our dynamics, with
this only distinction that now

00:45:46.930 --> 00:45:51.810
the controls are the only
optimization variables.

00:45:51.810 --> 00:45:52.710
Yes?

00:45:52.710 --> 00:45:55.370
Can you give an
example of problems

00:45:55.370 --> 00:45:59.130
in which you would use
control parametrization

00:45:59.130 --> 00:46:00.710
versus the previous one?

00:46:00.710 --> 00:46:01.210
Yeah.

00:46:01.210 --> 00:46:08.130
So in general, I would say
that-- so note that well before,

00:46:08.130 --> 00:46:13.070
if we wanted to include
some fancy state control--

00:46:13.070 --> 00:46:16.610
so a state constraint, so
a constraint on the x--

00:46:16.610 --> 00:46:19.330
since x is an explicit
optimization variable,

00:46:19.330 --> 00:46:21.230
we could have just
added that constraint.

00:46:21.230 --> 00:46:24.030
So say, x has to be
less or equal than 3,

00:46:24.030 --> 00:46:25.530
we could just add
another constraint

00:46:25.530 --> 00:46:27.630
saying x has to be less
than or equal than 3.

00:46:31.090 --> 00:46:36.740
In this other formulation, the
states are recursively computed,

00:46:36.740 --> 00:46:38.240
and so handling
state constraints

00:46:38.240 --> 00:46:40.960
is much more challenging.

00:46:40.960 --> 00:46:45.280
And so, at a very
high level more

00:46:45.280 --> 00:46:47.640
generally, I would say that
if you have complex state

00:46:47.640 --> 00:46:50.480
constraints, then I would
go for the state and control

00:46:50.480 --> 00:46:51.840
parametrization.

00:46:51.840 --> 00:46:57.597
If you don't, then this could
be a viable option, for example.

00:46:57.597 --> 00:47:01.080
[INAUDIBLE]

00:47:01.080 --> 00:47:05.800
Well in this case, both
would be able to [INAUDIBLE].

00:47:05.800 --> 00:47:11.080
But if you have control
constraints that you can only

00:47:11.080 --> 00:47:14.760
do step function changes, or you
can only continue with that t.

00:47:14.760 --> 00:47:17.480
I would say, from the
perspective of the optimizer,

00:47:17.480 --> 00:47:18.700
nothing would change here.

00:47:29.760 --> 00:47:33.430
All right, so this is
indeed exactly what we do.

00:47:33.430 --> 00:47:36.550
So we transcribe through the
control parametrization method

00:47:36.550 --> 00:47:40.070
the continuous time problem
into a discrete time problem.

00:47:40.070 --> 00:47:43.730
So let's see how this
works in the code.

00:47:50.350 --> 00:47:51.310
Yes.

00:47:51.310 --> 00:47:54.110
OK, so the definition
of the problem

00:47:54.110 --> 00:47:55.650
is exactly the same as before.

00:47:55.650 --> 00:47:59.070
So we have the same goal point,
we start from the same origin

00:47:59.070 --> 00:48:03.310
point, we have the same time
horizon and final time, tf.

00:48:03.310 --> 00:48:07.410
The dynamics of the flow of
the river is exactly the same.

00:48:11.310 --> 00:48:13.590
And yeah, so note
that now we define

00:48:13.590 --> 00:48:17.810
this dynamics function, which
indeed encodes our dynamic.

00:48:17.810 --> 00:48:22.230
So the fact that the next
x will be the previous x

00:48:22.230 --> 00:48:26.310
plus the flow of the river.

00:48:26.310 --> 00:48:28.580
We have some bounds
on our controls

00:48:28.580 --> 00:48:29.840
exactly as we did before.

00:48:29.840 --> 00:48:34.140
So u has to be between
minus umax and umax.

00:48:34.140 --> 00:48:37.780
But now the crux is in how
we define the constraints.

00:48:37.780 --> 00:48:41.620
So note that now the constraints
function is only a function of u

00:48:41.620 --> 00:48:43.500
because u is the
only optimization

00:48:43.500 --> 00:48:45.180
variable that we have.

00:48:45.180 --> 00:48:48.180
And so, what we do here is
we have this inner loop where

00:48:48.180 --> 00:48:51.140
we are propagating
our dynamics forward,

00:48:51.140 --> 00:49:00.820
where for all of our controls
in candidate solution u,

00:49:00.820 --> 00:49:03.160
we essentially propagate
the dynamics forward

00:49:03.160 --> 00:49:05.580
so we get a next state.

00:49:05.580 --> 00:49:11.940
And then we append these
constraints to the state

00:49:11.940 --> 00:49:12.940
at every time.

00:49:12.940 --> 00:49:14.380
Note that specifically
here we're

00:49:14.380 --> 00:49:18.380
using inequality
constraints, whereby in order

00:49:18.380 --> 00:49:21.740
to encode the equality
constraint that the final state

00:49:21.740 --> 00:49:25.380
has to be equal to ml,
we have both the fact

00:49:25.380 --> 00:49:28.360
that the state has to be
less than or equal than ml,

00:49:28.360 --> 00:49:31.780
and that state has to be
greater or equal than ml.

00:49:34.400 --> 00:49:37.180
But you can probably-- so
given what we're seeing here,

00:49:37.180 --> 00:49:40.920
you can imagine that the
sensitivity of this is much

00:49:40.920 --> 00:49:43.840
greater because
we are basically--

00:49:43.840 --> 00:49:47.520
the optimization variables
cannot be independently tweaked

00:49:47.520 --> 00:49:50.280
and snapped to respect
the constraints.

00:49:50.280 --> 00:49:53.600
But the influence
of the controls

00:49:53.600 --> 00:49:56.440
on the overall
trajectory is accounted

00:49:56.440 --> 00:50:00.780
for through this
propagation of the dynamics.

00:50:11.800 --> 00:50:18.440
So what we have now is that we
encode our initial guess, which

00:50:18.440 --> 00:50:23.360
again is something pretty
random in this case,

00:50:23.360 --> 00:50:27.190
and we have everything
to solve our problem.

00:50:27.190 --> 00:50:34.890
And if we run this now
with the umax equal to 1--

00:50:34.890 --> 00:50:37.030
so the same case as
before-- what we get

00:50:37.030 --> 00:50:38.990
is the exact same
solution, so it's

00:50:38.990 --> 00:50:43.750
still able to solve
this successfully.

00:50:43.750 --> 00:50:48.690
And if we run the problem
with umax equal to 0.75,

00:50:48.690 --> 00:50:51.870
we get that this method
indeed does solve it directly.

00:50:51.870 --> 00:50:56.310
So again, don't abstract
general conclusions

00:50:56.310 --> 00:50:57.910
from this specific run.

00:50:57.910 --> 00:51:01.030
But in general, I would say
that the control parametrization

00:51:01.030 --> 00:51:06.770
method is less dependent on the
quality of the initial guess.

00:51:06.770 --> 00:51:11.150
But this is very
much case dependent.

00:51:11.150 --> 00:51:12.110
Yes?

00:51:12.110 --> 00:51:14.270
Did it matter at
all whether you use

00:51:14.270 --> 00:51:16.850
control dependent versus
control and sync dependent?

00:51:16.850 --> 00:51:22.358
Because you just wrapped up
the state into the control.

00:51:22.358 --> 00:51:24.900
I guess I'm now confused this
particular time, because oh, it

00:51:24.900 --> 00:51:25.420
does matter.

00:51:25.420 --> 00:51:27.300
Because you have
state insurance,

00:51:27.300 --> 00:51:29.100
and that's important
to x, y, and z.

00:51:29.100 --> 00:51:31.760
But in this case where you
kind of have state insurance,

00:51:31.760 --> 00:51:34.300
depending on how you want to
look at it, it didn't matter.

00:51:34.300 --> 00:51:36.755
So I guess if you can clarify.

00:51:36.755 --> 00:51:37.880
Yeah, it's a good question.

00:51:37.880 --> 00:51:40.200
Now imagine that here--

00:51:40.200 --> 00:51:46.860
I hope that you agree with me
when we say that here, this

00:51:46.860 --> 00:51:49.900
is something that the solver
needs to run internally

00:51:49.900 --> 00:51:52.700
every time it wants to
evaluate some constraints.

00:51:52.700 --> 00:51:56.205
So take the solver
perspective on this.

00:51:56.205 --> 00:51:57.580
We have something--
we don't know

00:51:57.580 --> 00:52:00.100
what it is-- an optimization
problem that we want to solve,

00:52:00.100 --> 00:52:02.300
and the only knobs we
have to solve this problem

00:52:02.300 --> 00:52:04.260
are our optimization variables.

00:52:04.260 --> 00:52:06.020
In this formulation,
the only knobs

00:52:06.020 --> 00:52:08.020
that we have are the controls.

00:52:08.020 --> 00:52:12.060
And to evaluate how
any tweak in my knobs

00:52:12.060 --> 00:52:15.720
affects the feasibility
and quality of my solution,

00:52:15.720 --> 00:52:18.820
here I need to propagate
forward all of my dynamics.

00:52:18.820 --> 00:52:22.170
Which is different from the
previous transcription, where

00:52:22.170 --> 00:52:24.170
we could turn-- our knobs
were both the states

00:52:24.170 --> 00:52:27.270
and the controls, and the-- yes,
the states and the controls,

00:52:27.270 --> 00:52:30.850
and we could verify that
in a way more directly.

00:52:30.850 --> 00:52:33.750
And so, the moment
where we have--

00:52:33.750 --> 00:52:37.090
in this case, constraints are
pretty simple, mostly bounds.

00:52:37.090 --> 00:52:40.270
But the moment we have more
sophisticated state constraints,

00:52:40.270 --> 00:52:47.830
you can imagine how this becomes
much more sensitive to my knobs,

00:52:47.830 --> 00:52:49.410
which are my controls.

00:52:49.410 --> 00:52:51.330
Does it make sense,
more or less?

00:52:51.330 --> 00:52:54.930
So I guess my
confusion comes where

00:52:54.930 --> 00:52:57.530
it seems like as long as it
doesn't get too complicated,

00:52:57.530 --> 00:52:59.410
this one's better
because this is not

00:52:59.410 --> 00:53:04.970
as susceptible to a
bad initial guess, so.

00:53:04.970 --> 00:53:08.050
So I don't want you to take
this specific statement

00:53:08.050 --> 00:53:10.350
and generalize it to
any possible problem.

00:53:10.350 --> 00:53:12.410
For this problem, we
saw that initialization

00:53:12.410 --> 00:53:13.570
didn't matter too much.

00:53:13.570 --> 00:53:16.010
I would say in
general, maybe there

00:53:16.010 --> 00:53:18.610
is a slight tendency
towards initialization

00:53:18.610 --> 00:53:22.000
being less relevant here, but in
general it's very much problem

00:53:22.000 --> 00:53:22.500
dependent.

00:53:22.500 --> 00:53:24.333
I'm just thinking in
terms of rule of thumb,

00:53:24.333 --> 00:53:31.760
is it better to use this
state and control constraints,

00:53:31.760 --> 00:53:32.940
and do it that way.

00:53:32.940 --> 00:53:36.117
And then if you know there are
really no state constraints,

00:53:36.117 --> 00:53:38.200
then go this way, or is
it better to go this way--

00:53:38.200 --> 00:53:39.200
Yeah, if you would like.

00:53:39.200 --> 00:53:43.040
As a rule of thumb, if you have
simple or non-state constraints,

00:53:43.040 --> 00:53:44.583
then maybe you could try this.

00:53:44.583 --> 00:53:46.000
If you have complex
ones, then you

00:53:46.000 --> 00:53:47.720
might want to go
for the other one.

00:53:47.720 --> 00:53:51.600
Yes?

00:53:51.600 --> 00:53:53.920
So this is good because it
guarantees the trajectory is

00:53:53.920 --> 00:53:55.240
physically feasible.

00:53:55.240 --> 00:53:57.280
Because the dynamics
are like, all the states

00:53:57.280 --> 00:53:59.240
are propagated forward
based on the control.

00:53:59.240 --> 00:54:01.760
Yeah, there is some
element of that for sure,

00:54:01.760 --> 00:54:05.480
in the sense that
dynamics are implicitly

00:54:05.480 --> 00:54:09.800
encoding the fact that no
matter how I tune my knobs,

00:54:09.800 --> 00:54:12.240
the state trajectory
that I get is feasible.

00:54:12.240 --> 00:54:16.440
So there is some element
of that, for sure.

00:54:16.440 --> 00:54:19.030
Does the benefit of
one or the other kind

00:54:19.030 --> 00:54:22.070
depend a lot on the constraints?

00:54:22.070 --> 00:54:25.070
Yes, I would say it depends
a lot on the constraints,

00:54:25.070 --> 00:54:28.550
and in general, the
problem setting, yeah.

00:54:28.550 --> 00:54:31.270
So one thing I said
at the beginning

00:54:31.270 --> 00:54:33.890
is that this parametrization
has fewer variables,

00:54:33.890 --> 00:54:36.510
and so I hinted at the fact
that they were just smaller

00:54:36.510 --> 00:54:38.190
optimization problems.

00:54:38.190 --> 00:54:42.150
Of course it is smaller, but I
am propagating these dynamics

00:54:42.150 --> 00:54:46.110
at every time, so, trade-offs.

00:54:46.110 --> 00:54:48.670
I think there was-- yeah?

00:54:48.670 --> 00:54:51.290
So this all depends on
a numerical propagation.

00:54:51.290 --> 00:54:55.210
How often would you say is
the selection of step size

00:54:55.210 --> 00:54:56.630
for these types
of optimizations?

00:54:56.630 --> 00:54:59.310
Yeah, so a great
follow-up from what

00:54:59.310 --> 00:55:01.350
we were just
saying, because this

00:55:01.350 --> 00:55:03.970
might seem as a smaller
optimization problem.

00:55:03.970 --> 00:55:07.430
But then in this case, since
I'm integrating the dynamics

00:55:07.430 --> 00:55:09.150
forward, then I might
have to-- in order

00:55:09.150 --> 00:55:13.830
to solve my problem
accurately, I

00:55:13.830 --> 00:55:17.430
might need to reduce my step
size and discretization.

00:55:17.430 --> 00:55:22.250
And so, this would in turn be a
bigger optimization problem, so.

00:55:22.250 --> 00:55:24.290
Also a problem
depending matter, but it

00:55:24.290 --> 00:55:27.310
could matter, discretization.

00:55:27.310 --> 00:55:30.250
And also, not only the time
discretization, but also the way

00:55:30.250 --> 00:55:32.150
I integrate my dynamics.

00:55:32.150 --> 00:55:36.810
Because in the state space,
what we're essentially saying

00:55:36.810 --> 00:55:41.530
is that we have a
piecewise linear state

00:55:41.530 --> 00:55:43.930
trajectory that we
are approximating

00:55:43.930 --> 00:55:46.210
the continuous time
with one width.

00:55:46.210 --> 00:55:50.230
And so, the bigger
the step size,

00:55:50.230 --> 00:55:53.130
the more I have chances
of maybe violating

00:55:53.130 --> 00:55:57.978
constraints in between,
and things like that.

00:55:57.978 --> 00:55:59.270
Does that answer your question?

00:56:02.570 --> 00:56:03.390
Oh, sorry.

00:56:03.390 --> 00:56:05.810
Yeah?

00:56:05.810 --> 00:56:08.690
--or more complicated
cost function.

00:56:08.690 --> 00:56:14.320
Those are the SciPy [INAUDIBLE]
optimizer do the same.

00:56:14.320 --> 00:56:16.760
Optimizers do matter a lot.

00:56:16.760 --> 00:56:19.680
Here we were using
the most standard

00:56:19.680 --> 00:56:21.840
out there, but definitely.

00:56:21.840 --> 00:56:24.380
Also, within the
SciPy ecosystem,

00:56:24.380 --> 00:56:27.040
there are a number of different
optimizers you can choose.

00:56:27.040 --> 00:56:31.080
But this is also what I
meant with we are shifting

00:56:31.080 --> 00:56:34.360
mental effort from formulation
of the problem and optimality

00:56:34.360 --> 00:56:38.420
conditions to the solution of a
black box optimization problem.

00:56:38.420 --> 00:56:42.440
And within this umbrella
term, what I mean

00:56:42.440 --> 00:56:45.620
is that also the choice of
optimizer does play a role.

00:56:53.240 --> 00:56:53.740
All right.

00:56:53.740 --> 00:56:57.600
So basically, we saw these
two families of transcription

00:56:57.600 --> 00:57:00.640
methods where at a high
level, what we want to achieve

00:57:00.640 --> 00:57:02.420
is exactly the same thing.

00:57:02.420 --> 00:57:05.320
So we want to go from our
continuous time optimal control

00:57:05.320 --> 00:57:07.800
problem formulation
to discrete time

00:57:07.800 --> 00:57:13.030
1, which we can solve through
black box numerical solvers.

00:57:13.030 --> 00:57:17.250
We saw the control
parameterization methods,

00:57:17.250 --> 00:57:20.590
which entails using only
the controls as optimization

00:57:20.590 --> 00:57:23.290
variables, and
then in this case,

00:57:23.290 --> 00:57:25.870
the dynamics are
essentially used

00:57:25.870 --> 00:57:30.830
to recursively propagate forward
in time my state trajectory.

00:57:30.830 --> 00:57:33.990
And we saw the state and
control parameterization method

00:57:33.990 --> 00:57:37.670
whereby both states and controls
are optimization variables,

00:57:37.670 --> 00:57:40.230
and these two
optimization variables

00:57:40.230 --> 00:57:42.430
are linked through
the dynamics, which

00:57:42.430 --> 00:57:45.850
become constraints that link
these two variables together.

00:57:50.230 --> 00:57:53.070
Of course, here we're just
scratching the surface

00:57:53.070 --> 00:57:57.310
on the types of approaches
that can be used.

00:57:57.310 --> 00:57:59.910
In general, I think, as your
colleague was also alluding to,

00:57:59.910 --> 00:58:03.010
there are definitely hybrids
of these two methods.

00:58:03.010 --> 00:58:05.830
For example, multiple shooting
is one that I definitely

00:58:05.830 --> 00:58:08.430
wanted to mention.

00:58:08.430 --> 00:58:11.980
So if at a high
level, it's clear

00:58:11.980 --> 00:58:15.620
what we are doing in the
control parametrization

00:58:15.620 --> 00:58:20.140
methods with this shooting of
the dynamics forward in time.

00:58:20.140 --> 00:58:23.940
In multiple shooting, this is
basically a hybrid in the sense

00:58:23.940 --> 00:58:26.180
that rather than
propagating forward

00:58:26.180 --> 00:58:28.180
my dynamics from the
initial condition

00:58:28.180 --> 00:58:30.700
to the final condition,
what I'm doing is

00:58:30.700 --> 00:58:36.300
I'm doing this single shooting
on a smaller subset of my time

00:58:36.300 --> 00:58:37.180
intervals.

00:58:37.180 --> 00:58:39.180
So you could imagine
something where

00:58:39.180 --> 00:58:41.140
I'm propagating my
dynamics forward

00:58:41.140 --> 00:58:44.780
in time only for some
subset of the time,

00:58:44.780 --> 00:58:48.580
and then I'm doing this again,
and then I'm doing this again.

00:58:48.580 --> 00:58:54.780
And we introduce optimization
variables at these dislocation

00:58:54.780 --> 00:58:57.900
times, and the dynamics
become constraints

00:58:57.900 --> 00:59:01.900
where I want to tie these
two endpoints together.

00:59:01.900 --> 00:59:04.100
Again, high level picture,
but what I meant here

00:59:04.100 --> 00:59:07.450
is that there are some
hybridizations of these two

00:59:07.450 --> 00:59:08.110
approaches.

00:59:08.110 --> 00:59:08.610
Yes?

00:59:08.610 --> 00:59:11.490
So will that allow you
to be a little bit more

00:59:11.490 --> 00:59:15.410
specific on state constraints
around those joining points?

00:59:15.410 --> 00:59:18.330
In turn, you would
get that, yeah.

00:59:18.330 --> 00:59:23.010
Of course, depending on how
big your single interval is,

00:59:23.010 --> 00:59:24.610
you might have
the same problems.

00:59:24.610 --> 00:59:25.750
But in general, yes.

00:59:29.090 --> 00:59:31.570
And of course, also, another
discussion that came up

00:59:31.570 --> 00:59:35.890
is that here we were assuming
a simple Euler discretization

00:59:35.890 --> 00:59:37.850
and integration of our dynamics.

00:59:37.850 --> 00:59:40.970
We could go for more
sophisticated approximations.

00:59:40.970 --> 00:59:44.330
For example,
leveraging polynomials

00:59:44.330 --> 00:59:47.610
which lead to a kind of
Hermite-Simpson collocation

00:59:47.610 --> 00:59:50.970
schemes and
pseudospectral methods.

00:59:50.970 --> 00:59:54.590
And so, this is an entire
family of approaches.

00:59:57.570 --> 01:00:01.430
Any questions before I move
on the last topic for today?

01:00:11.080 --> 01:00:11.940
OK, great.

01:00:11.940 --> 01:00:14.400
I mean, I think it's pretty--

01:00:14.400 --> 01:00:16.880
I hope it's clear,
the attractiveness

01:00:16.880 --> 01:00:18.160
that direct methods have.

01:00:18.160 --> 01:00:21.960
Because we had to
spend multiple lectures

01:00:21.960 --> 01:00:24.800
into deriving these
optimality conditions

01:00:24.800 --> 01:00:28.480
before going into solving them
through numerical methods.

01:00:28.480 --> 01:00:30.780
Here, we're basically
defining the formulation,

01:00:30.780 --> 01:00:33.920
taking that at face value,
discretizing it, and throwing it

01:00:33.920 --> 01:00:36.260
to numerical
optimization methods.

01:00:39.120 --> 01:00:40.760
So for the last
topic for today is

01:00:40.760 --> 01:00:42.360
sequential convex
programming, which

01:00:42.360 --> 01:00:46.960
is arguably actually one of
my favorite direct methods.

01:00:46.960 --> 01:00:50.520
Whereby the idea of
sequential convex programming

01:00:50.520 --> 01:00:52.560
is that until now,
what we're doing

01:00:52.560 --> 01:00:56.840
is that we're taking the
continuous time formulation,

01:00:56.840 --> 01:01:00.400
discretizing it into a
nonlinear optimization problem,

01:01:00.400 --> 01:01:06.020
and then solving it
through nonlinear solvers.

01:01:06.020 --> 01:01:08.220
Of course, these nonlinear
optimization problems

01:01:08.220 --> 01:01:10.420
might be challenging to solve.

01:01:10.420 --> 01:01:13.020
And so, sequential convex
programming essentially

01:01:13.020 --> 01:01:18.980
defines an iterative strategy to
solve this potentially complex

01:01:18.980 --> 01:01:22.620
nonlinear optimization problem.

01:01:22.620 --> 01:01:25.060
And the way it does
this is that we

01:01:25.060 --> 01:01:29.540
will be defining, as
the name alludes to,

01:01:29.540 --> 01:01:34.620
a sequence of convex problems
which would ultimately

01:01:34.620 --> 01:01:38.460
and iteratively lead to the
solution of the actual nonlinear

01:01:38.460 --> 01:01:40.780
problem, which we
care about solving.

01:01:40.780 --> 01:01:43.020
Why we care about doing
this is because you

01:01:43.020 --> 01:01:46.360
did a recitation last week
about convex optimization.

01:01:46.360 --> 01:01:49.460
Convex optimization is great
because we can solve it easily,

01:01:49.460 --> 01:01:54.220
fast, and we are guaranteed
to get the global optimum.

01:01:54.220 --> 01:01:56.100
And specifically,
the way we do this

01:01:56.100 --> 01:02:00.580
is by introducing essentially--

01:02:00.580 --> 01:02:04.010
introducing a surrogate
problem of our original problem

01:02:04.010 --> 01:02:05.290
that we wish to solve.

01:02:05.290 --> 01:02:07.330
And the surrogate
problem is essentially

01:02:07.330 --> 01:02:11.770
a version where we are
linearizing and convexifying

01:02:11.770 --> 01:02:17.850
the nonlinear elements in
our optimization problem.

01:02:17.850 --> 01:02:21.210
And specifically, where the
non-linearities come from

01:02:21.210 --> 01:02:26.330
are either usually the
dynamics, or the cost.

01:02:26.330 --> 01:02:28.770
So the idea of sequential
convex programming

01:02:28.770 --> 01:02:33.890
is to linearize and convexify
the dynamics and the cost

01:02:33.890 --> 01:02:36.930
around nominal trajectories.

01:02:36.930 --> 01:02:40.570
Solve this problem, get
a new nominal trajectory,

01:02:40.570 --> 01:02:43.530
re-convexify, re-linearize,
and keep iterating this

01:02:43.530 --> 01:02:46.610
until we get a better solution.

01:02:46.610 --> 01:02:50.970
But let's look at this a
little bit more in detail.

01:02:50.970 --> 01:02:55.030
Any questions on the high
level perspective here?

01:02:58.130 --> 01:03:00.600
Suppose the state [INAUDIBLE]?

01:03:04.280 --> 01:03:06.600
The question is, what is
the difference between--

01:03:06.600 --> 01:03:07.720
[INAUDIBLE]

01:03:07.720 --> 01:03:10.200
--constantly relinearizing
and then you're resolving,

01:03:10.200 --> 01:03:12.640
and you're breaking your choice.

01:03:12.640 --> 01:03:16.280
So here we would
basically break down--

01:03:16.280 --> 01:03:19.400
so here we would be doing
one optimization solve,

01:03:19.400 --> 01:03:22.340
and we're breaking down
the trajectory over time,

01:03:22.340 --> 01:03:22.840
essentially.

01:03:22.840 --> 01:03:25.740
We have these chunks
over time here,

01:03:25.740 --> 01:03:28.220
and it will be clear now
because we will go through it.

01:03:28.220 --> 01:03:32.280
But here we are
not chopping time,

01:03:32.280 --> 01:03:36.060
but we're just solving different
surrogate problems over time.

01:03:36.060 --> 01:03:38.000
So here is one single solve.

01:03:38.000 --> 01:03:40.640
Here will be n solves.

01:03:40.640 --> 01:03:45.000
But here, the single solve is
a solve of a nonlinear program.

01:03:45.000 --> 01:03:49.665
Here we will be solving
recursively convex programs.

01:03:49.665 --> 01:03:50.540
Does that make sense?

01:03:56.000 --> 01:04:00.270
And so, the general
idea is that we are--

01:04:00.270 --> 01:04:05.190
so assume that g, so the
cost is convex for now.

01:04:05.190 --> 01:04:10.590
And let x0 u0 be a
nominal trajectory

01:04:10.590 --> 01:04:13.070
for our states and controls.

01:04:13.070 --> 01:04:19.990
In this slide, the indices
represent iteration steps

01:04:19.990 --> 01:04:22.670
where-- remember, this is
an iterative algorithm,

01:04:22.670 --> 01:04:31.230
so here 0 would be the initial
guess at iteration 0, which

01:04:31.230 --> 01:04:34.830
will lead to a surrogate
problem which we will solve

01:04:34.830 --> 01:04:37.590
to get a nominal guess
at iteration one,

01:04:37.590 --> 01:04:39.630
and keep iterating over that.

01:04:39.630 --> 01:04:45.890
So let x0 u0 be a nominal
trajectory, or more concretely--

01:04:53.830 --> 01:04:59.260
so x0t would essentially be--

01:04:59.260 --> 01:05:04.852
with t between t0 and tf--

01:05:04.852 --> 01:05:06.420
is a full function.

01:05:06.420 --> 01:05:10.140
So we assume we have a
nominal state trajectory

01:05:10.140 --> 01:05:13.760
and a nominal control signal.

01:05:20.460 --> 01:05:23.563
So the way we want
to linearize--

01:05:23.563 --> 01:05:24.980
so remember, what
we're doing here

01:05:24.980 --> 01:05:26.605
is we are taking the
nonlinear program,

01:05:26.605 --> 01:05:29.840
and we want to turn it into a
convex optimization problem,

01:05:29.840 --> 01:05:35.460
and we do this by linearizing
the nonlinear dynamics.

01:05:35.460 --> 01:05:37.820
And specifically, we
linearize these dynamics

01:05:37.820 --> 01:05:40.580
around this nominal trajectory.

01:05:40.580 --> 01:05:44.080
So we do this as usual
through Taylor expansion.

01:05:44.080 --> 01:05:51.580
So what we can do, we
know is that f of x ut

01:05:51.580 --> 01:05:55.810
can be approximated
as f evaluated

01:05:55.810 --> 01:06:06.930
at the nominal trajectory
x0 u0 t plus the Jacobian,

01:06:06.930 --> 01:06:10.770
the derivative of the
function f with respect to x

01:06:10.770 --> 01:06:13.350
times the difference.

01:06:13.350 --> 01:06:17.610
So x minus x0 plus
the derivative

01:06:17.610 --> 01:06:21.250
with respect to f of
f with respect to u

01:06:21.250 --> 01:06:25.130
times u minus u0 plus.

01:06:25.130 --> 01:06:30.750
Here we would have
higher order terms.

01:06:30.750 --> 01:06:31.250
Yeah?

01:06:31.250 --> 01:06:35.610
Do you have x amount in u0 of t?

01:06:35.610 --> 01:06:40.090
I always thought
x0 is at t0, but.

01:06:40.090 --> 01:06:43.250
So what is x0 at
a t0 equal to t0?

01:06:43.250 --> 01:06:45.230
So here we are introducing this.

01:06:45.230 --> 01:06:48.730
So for the upcoming
two slides, the indices

01:06:48.730 --> 01:06:52.170
represent iteration steps
because we are introducing

01:06:52.170 --> 01:06:53.950
this iterative algorithm.

01:06:53.950 --> 01:06:59.530
So for us, x0 t is a full
function at iteration step 0.

01:06:59.530 --> 01:07:03.350
So it's like the x of
the tf, [INAUDIBLE]?

01:07:03.350 --> 01:07:04.690
I take the x?

01:07:04.690 --> 01:07:09.330
OK, so that x0 on that
side is not the same x0.

01:07:09.330 --> 01:07:09.870
Which one?

01:07:09.870 --> 01:07:15.230
We have x and 0 is x0, but
that is not the same x0

01:07:15.230 --> 01:07:16.847
that you're writing.

01:07:16.847 --> 01:07:17.930
And then you have x of tf.

01:07:17.930 --> 01:07:18.730
Yeah, you're right.

01:07:18.730 --> 01:07:19.770
It's confusing.

01:07:19.770 --> 01:07:21.990
Yeah, I will update it.

01:07:21.990 --> 01:07:23.330
But is it clear what we are--

01:07:23.330 --> 01:07:23.970
Yes, yes.

01:07:23.970 --> 01:07:26.810
I just was a little
confused, but I got you now.

01:07:29.910 --> 01:07:32.590
And so, here, what we
did is we basically

01:07:32.590 --> 01:07:36.150
linearized the dynamics
around a nominal trajectory.

01:07:36.150 --> 01:07:40.030
And so, basically what we
get is that now g is convex,

01:07:40.030 --> 01:07:43.350
we have now linear
dynamics, and so

01:07:43.350 --> 01:07:50.030
we basically get a new
problem, which is now linear.

01:07:50.030 --> 01:07:53.640
And so what we would do now
is, we solve this new problem,

01:07:53.640 --> 01:07:56.260
we would get a nominal trajec--

01:07:56.260 --> 01:07:58.140
a new candidate
nominal trajectory,

01:07:58.140 --> 01:08:00.740
x1 u1, where again,
here the indices

01:08:00.740 --> 01:08:02.700
represent iteration steps.

01:08:02.700 --> 01:08:05.380
And we would then just
repeat this process

01:08:05.380 --> 01:08:10.300
whereby we would now
re-linearize our dynamics

01:08:10.300 --> 01:08:13.280
around this updated
nominal trajectory,

01:08:13.280 --> 01:08:18.760
solve the linear problem, and
iterate until we, for example,

01:08:18.760 --> 01:08:22.600
stop improving our solution.

01:08:26.700 --> 01:08:29.020
Of course, in
between these steps

01:08:29.020 --> 01:08:32.380
we always have to discretize
because we're doing these

01:08:32.380 --> 01:08:33.920
through direct methods.

01:08:33.920 --> 01:08:38.420
And so at every time we would
be linearizing the dynamics,

01:08:38.420 --> 01:08:40.939
we now have a linear
optimal control problem.

01:08:40.939 --> 01:08:44.180
To solve this, we discretize
it exactly as we did,

01:08:44.180 --> 01:08:47.340
which boils down into a
convex optimization problem.

01:08:47.340 --> 01:08:51.370
Solve this and keep iterating.

01:08:51.370 --> 01:08:54.130
So at a high level, sequential
convex programming essentially

01:08:54.130 --> 01:08:56.250
defines the scheme
where we're given

01:08:56.250 --> 01:09:00.050
a nonlinear optimal control
problem in continuous time.

01:09:00.050 --> 01:09:03.970
To solve this, we define
this iterative scheme

01:09:03.970 --> 01:09:06.930
whereby we linearize
the dynamics

01:09:06.930 --> 01:09:09.350
around a nominal trajectory.

01:09:09.350 --> 01:09:12.569
Initially, this would
just be an initial guess.

01:09:12.569 --> 01:09:16.609
This would then lead us into
defining a continuous time,

01:09:16.609 --> 01:09:21.850
but linear optimal control
problem, which we discretize

01:09:21.850 --> 01:09:25.649
in time, which leads to a convex
optimization problem, which

01:09:25.649 --> 01:09:27.710
we can solve
reliably, efficiently,

01:09:27.710 --> 01:09:31.770
and to the global minimum,
which leads to having an updated

01:09:31.770 --> 01:09:32.870
nominal trajectory.

01:09:32.870 --> 01:09:37.210
And we keep iterating
on this loop

01:09:37.210 --> 01:09:40.109
until some termination
criteria is reached.

01:09:42.930 --> 01:09:44.649
Does it make sense
what we're doing?

01:09:49.359 --> 01:09:52.120
Yeah, so basically what
we're exploiting here

01:09:52.120 --> 01:09:57.400
is the fact that we are
given a nominal trajectory.

01:09:57.400 --> 01:10:01.040
this would be x0.

01:10:01.040 --> 01:10:03.360
We exploit the fact
that we can basically

01:10:03.360 --> 01:10:07.880
linearize our nonlinear
dynamics around this trajectory,

01:10:07.880 --> 01:10:12.600
meaning we exploit the
fact that deviations

01:10:12.600 --> 01:10:16.200
around this trajectory
essentially behave--

01:10:16.200 --> 01:10:20.720
can be well approximated
by some linear dynamics.

01:10:20.720 --> 01:10:24.760
And we use these linear
dynamics to essentially define

01:10:24.760 --> 01:10:28.280
linear optimal control problem,
whereby this would lead us

01:10:28.280 --> 01:10:32.960
to maybe an updated
guess, say, x1

01:10:32.960 --> 01:10:35.960
in this case, which
essentially is

01:10:35.960 --> 01:10:39.260
like a minor tweak around
our original solution,

01:10:39.260 --> 01:10:41.260
hopefully in the
right direction.

01:10:41.260 --> 01:10:43.880
And we keep iterating this
and refining our solution

01:10:43.880 --> 01:10:48.710
until we are satisfied
with our solution.

01:10:48.710 --> 01:10:49.430
Yes?

01:10:49.430 --> 01:10:52.190
You talk about your update
for your new function

01:10:52.190 --> 01:10:55.610
when you're talking about the
update for the new guesses.

01:10:55.610 --> 01:10:58.990
So u1, u2, 3, 4.

01:10:58.990 --> 01:11:03.150
How is that exactly set, per se?

01:11:03.150 --> 01:11:04.410
Can you repeat the question?

01:11:04.410 --> 01:11:04.910
Yeah.

01:11:04.910 --> 01:11:08.070
So you're iterating in your
function, which I'm assuming--

01:11:08.070 --> 01:11:13.130
is that function also changing
the x for you and the--

01:11:13.130 --> 01:11:13.630
Yes.

01:11:13.630 --> 01:11:16.130
So this is only in state space.

01:11:16.130 --> 01:11:18.490
We would be doing this
also for the controls.

01:11:18.490 --> 01:11:18.990
Yeah?

01:11:18.990 --> 01:11:20.070
[INAUDIBLE]

01:11:20.070 --> 01:11:21.830
--changing everything.

01:11:21.830 --> 01:11:25.510
It's a function that changes
both the state and the control.

01:11:25.510 --> 01:11:26.350
Yes.

01:11:26.350 --> 01:11:28.330
I'm not sure what you
mean by function exactly,

01:11:28.330 --> 01:11:30.070
but what I'm saying
here is, I was

01:11:30.070 --> 01:11:33.630
representing the iterative
scheme in state space here.

01:11:33.630 --> 01:11:35.830
[INAUDIBLE]

01:11:35.830 --> 01:11:37.450
These are our dynamics.

01:11:37.450 --> 01:11:37.990
Yes?

01:11:37.990 --> 01:11:40.870
Yes, but the function is
describing all of your dynamics.

01:11:40.870 --> 01:11:42.870
And so, that is
just representing

01:11:42.870 --> 01:11:45.040
how you are going to be
changing the x and the u

01:11:45.040 --> 01:11:48.440
in each iteration.

01:11:48.440 --> 01:11:48.940
Not really.

01:11:48.940 --> 01:11:50.940
Among the iterations--
so these are just

01:11:50.940 --> 01:11:54.340
the dynamics of our system.

01:11:54.340 --> 01:11:57.100
What we do is we linearize
these dynamics in order

01:11:57.100 --> 01:11:59.980
to get a linear formulation.

01:11:59.980 --> 01:12:04.240
We solve this linear formulation
to get an updated trajectory.

01:12:04.240 --> 01:12:06.180
So what changes from--

01:12:06.180 --> 01:12:11.860
what brings us from x0 to x1
is one step in this solve.

01:12:11.860 --> 01:12:13.420
So we're solving
a convex problem,

01:12:13.420 --> 01:12:16.040
which leads to the
next nominal guess.

01:12:16.040 --> 01:12:18.630
[INAUDIBLE]

01:12:19.980 --> 01:12:21.440
--a new path and
a new controller.

01:12:21.440 --> 01:12:21.940
Yes.

01:12:21.940 --> 01:12:25.220
And then what we do is we
would be re-linearizing around

01:12:25.220 --> 01:12:30.660
the updated nominal trajectory,
and modifying this again

01:12:30.660 --> 01:12:32.400
to get an x2, and so forth.

01:12:36.140 --> 01:12:42.500
So I want to quickly show
this in code as well.

01:12:46.600 --> 01:12:49.880
So the usual problem.

01:12:49.880 --> 01:12:53.420
What changes here is that now
we are linearizing our dynamics.

01:12:53.420 --> 01:12:55.220
We're not doing
this analytically,

01:12:55.220 --> 01:12:58.180
we're using JAX to
linearize our dynamics.

01:12:58.180 --> 01:13:02.600
So we have this function here,
which essentially computes

01:13:02.600 --> 01:13:07.520
the Jacobians of our
dynamics-- so these two terms--

01:13:07.520 --> 01:13:09.420
which are essentially
two matrices.

01:13:09.420 --> 01:13:19.240
This would be our matrix A,
this would be our matrix B.

01:13:19.240 --> 01:13:21.440
And so we define the
linearized dynamics

01:13:21.440 --> 01:13:28.440
as the original dynamics
evaluated at the nominal guess.

01:13:28.440 --> 01:13:34.080
This one here plus
the derivative

01:13:34.080 --> 01:13:37.560
of f with respect to x
times the displacement in x

01:13:37.560 --> 01:13:39.680
plus the derivative of f
with respect to u times

01:13:39.680 --> 01:13:41.650
the displacement in u.

01:13:46.020 --> 01:13:47.990
And so, once we have
this, we basically

01:13:47.990 --> 01:13:53.850
define our sequential convex
programming iterations.

01:13:53.850 --> 01:13:56.990
So here we assume we have a
maximum number of iterations

01:13:56.990 --> 01:13:58.550
that is 100.

01:13:58.550 --> 01:14:02.110
So for each of our
iteration steps or loops,

01:14:02.110 --> 01:14:05.150
we would be defining an
optimal control problem which

01:14:05.150 --> 01:14:11.830
essentially entails our x's
and u's. For now, ignore the s.

01:14:11.830 --> 01:14:14.770
We have our controls, so
we have our cost function,

01:14:14.770 --> 01:14:18.950
we have our linearized dynamics,
we have our initial and terminal

01:14:18.950 --> 01:14:23.710
conditions, we have our state
and control constraints.

01:14:23.710 --> 01:14:28.790
And we can just
solve it, and we see

01:14:28.790 --> 01:14:32.770
that this leads to the
solution of the same problem.

01:14:36.150 --> 01:14:39.086
One thing-- yes?

01:14:39.086 --> 01:14:42.580
At the start, didn't we come
up with a nonlinear programming

01:14:42.580 --> 01:14:43.620
solution to it?

01:14:43.620 --> 01:14:45.820
I'm confused when
you want to use DCP

01:14:45.820 --> 01:14:47.500
instead of the original method.

01:14:47.500 --> 01:14:51.260
So the problem is that the
original nonlinear optimization

01:14:51.260 --> 01:14:53.740
problem might be just
very hard to solve.

01:14:53.740 --> 01:14:56.220
And so SCP is
basically a strategy

01:14:56.220 --> 01:14:58.980
to solve nonlinear
optimization problems, which

01:14:58.980 --> 01:15:03.660
relies in an inner loop on
a convex optimization, which

01:15:03.660 --> 01:15:05.100
is fast and reliable.

01:15:05.100 --> 01:15:08.660
And so you could see this
as a sophisticated way

01:15:08.660 --> 01:15:12.060
of solving those nonlinear
optimization problems, which

01:15:12.060 --> 01:15:13.300
is quite effective.

01:15:13.300 --> 01:15:15.740
And I would say, most
state-of-the-art approaches

01:15:15.740 --> 01:15:18.840
nowadays use something
along these flavors.

01:15:18.840 --> 01:15:19.340
Yeah?

01:15:19.340 --> 01:15:23.840
[INAUDIBLE]

01:15:24.840 --> 01:15:25.720
Yes?

01:15:25.720 --> 01:15:28.160
How do we know that
linearizing the dynamics

01:15:28.160 --> 01:15:30.660
and then using convex
optimization to solve

01:15:30.660 --> 01:15:33.680
each sub-problem will
get us closer to-- really

01:15:33.680 --> 01:15:34.980
get us a better solution?

01:15:34.980 --> 01:15:36.320
Yeah, it's a great question.

01:15:36.320 --> 01:15:40.130
And I would say-- so
this is also where most

01:15:40.130 --> 01:15:41.690
state-of-the-art--

01:15:41.690 --> 01:15:45.370
so what differentiates between
a naive implementation of SCP,

01:15:45.370 --> 01:15:48.290
and a state-of-the-art
implementation of SCP.

01:15:48.290 --> 01:15:49.970
So where this difference lies.

01:15:49.970 --> 01:15:51.190
And this is because--

01:15:54.710 --> 01:15:57.370
so at the overall
high level picture,

01:15:57.370 --> 01:15:59.930
SCP describes a
scheme where we are

01:15:59.930 --> 01:16:03.730
given an initial
trajectory guess somehow,

01:16:03.730 --> 01:16:06.970
we linearize the non-convexities
around this nominal guess

01:16:06.970 --> 01:16:08.410
as we just saw.

01:16:08.410 --> 01:16:10.210
Forget this box for now.

01:16:10.210 --> 01:16:13.590
We discretize it, we solve it
through convex optimization.

01:16:13.590 --> 01:16:15.190
If we're happy, then we're done.

01:16:15.190 --> 01:16:18.210
If we're not happy, we
start again this loop.

01:16:18.210 --> 01:16:20.010
Again, ignore this box.

01:16:20.010 --> 01:16:25.050
What I meant between difference
between naive implementation

01:16:25.050 --> 01:16:27.690
and a state-of-the-art
implementation exactly lies

01:16:27.690 --> 01:16:31.250
in these two boxes, whereby
since we are introducing this

01:16:31.250 --> 01:16:33.930
surrogate problem--
which is, on one hand,

01:16:33.930 --> 01:16:35.960
easy to solve
because it's convex--

01:16:35.960 --> 01:16:38.960
but it could introduce
some additional problems.

01:16:38.960 --> 01:16:41.760
And two specifically
relevant ones

01:16:41.760 --> 01:16:46.680
are artificial infeasibility
and artificial unboundedness.

01:16:46.680 --> 01:16:50.080
So since we're introducing
a surrogate problem,

01:16:50.080 --> 01:16:52.880
this surrogate problem
might be infeasible

01:16:52.880 --> 01:16:55.520
while the overall nonlinear
problem might actually

01:16:55.520 --> 01:16:57.160
be feasible.

01:16:57.160 --> 01:17:00.640
And the other problem,
artificial unboundedness,

01:17:00.640 --> 01:17:04.080
is that the convex problem might
just lead us into a situation

01:17:04.080 --> 01:17:06.760
where the optimal solution
of the convex problem

01:17:06.760 --> 01:17:09.892
just leads us to minus
infinity, for example.

01:17:09.892 --> 01:17:11.600
And so, there are
different ways in which

01:17:11.600 --> 01:17:14.720
one could approach this.

01:17:14.720 --> 01:17:18.920
So typically, to handle
artificial unboundedness,

01:17:18.920 --> 01:17:24.800
you want to ensure that in
solving the individual iterate,

01:17:24.800 --> 01:17:28.640
you don't diverge too much
from your nominal guess.

01:17:28.640 --> 01:17:33.030
And this is because since
we're linearizing the dynamics,

01:17:33.030 --> 01:17:35.670
this linearization is only
accurate in a neighborhood

01:17:35.670 --> 01:17:37.750
of the nominal trajectory.

01:17:37.750 --> 01:17:41.110
And so, this is where
concepts like trust regions

01:17:41.110 --> 01:17:45.590
are introduced in order to
basically massage the convex

01:17:45.590 --> 01:17:47.590
problem such that we
are not moving too much

01:17:47.590 --> 01:17:49.910
from the original solution.

01:17:49.910 --> 01:17:52.270
For example, trust
region constraint

01:17:52.270 --> 01:17:58.950
could be something
where I want to stay

01:17:58.950 --> 01:18:03.630
within a certain distance
from my nominal trajectory.

01:18:03.630 --> 01:18:08.510
And the other topic is the one
of artificial infeasibility.

01:18:08.510 --> 01:18:11.910
And typically, this is handled
through slack variables.

01:18:11.910 --> 01:18:14.950
So a slack variable
is something where--

01:18:14.950 --> 01:18:18.430
say we have a constraint
that our u has

01:18:18.430 --> 01:18:22.190
to be smaller than a umax.

01:18:22.190 --> 01:18:26.190
We could add an additional
optimization variable, s,

01:18:26.190 --> 01:18:31.570
which allows us to loosen
this constraint if needed,

01:18:31.570 --> 01:18:35.470
but we would be penalizing
this in the cost.

01:18:35.470 --> 01:18:39.250
So an updated cost could
be the original cost plus

01:18:39.250 --> 01:18:42.130
s squared, for example.

01:18:42.130 --> 01:18:44.090
Now this is kind
of a long detour,

01:18:44.090 --> 01:18:46.630
but does this go in the
direction of what you're asking?

01:18:49.250 --> 01:18:51.850
And so, now to
conclude, if you were

01:18:51.850 --> 01:18:54.370
seeing those s
variables before, this

01:18:54.370 --> 01:18:56.670
was indeed the slack variable.

01:18:56.670 --> 01:19:00.650
So we were introducing this
fictitious optimization variable

01:19:00.650 --> 01:19:07.250
s, which we are adding to
our bound constraints u,

01:19:07.250 --> 01:19:08.830
and in the cost.

01:19:08.830 --> 01:19:12.010
So we are penalizing
the slack variable.

01:19:12.010 --> 01:19:14.970
And if you look at the
results of the optimization,

01:19:14.970 --> 01:19:18.810
we see how the slack variable
indeed decreases over time.

01:19:18.810 --> 01:19:21.170
Because initially
for the optimization,

01:19:21.170 --> 01:19:24.610
apparently it is helpful to
violate some of the constraints.

01:19:24.610 --> 01:19:26.750
But then since we're
penalizing them,

01:19:26.750 --> 01:19:29.500
this reduces and
ultimately goes to 0.

01:19:34.040 --> 01:19:36.180
Any questions on this?

01:19:40.600 --> 01:19:43.560
OK, perfect.

01:19:43.560 --> 01:19:47.240
Yeah, just to wrap up, this
is a nice quote from Professor

01:19:47.240 --> 01:19:49.672
Tedrake at MIT, where
he essentially says,

01:19:49.672 --> 01:19:52.380
"as you begin to play with these
algorithms on your own problems,

01:19:52.380 --> 01:19:55.000
you might feel like you're on
an emotional roller coaster."

01:19:55.000 --> 01:19:57.900
And this is because, again,
as we were hinting at before,

01:19:57.900 --> 01:19:59.840
we are shifting a lot
of the mental effort

01:19:59.840 --> 01:20:02.800
into tweaking the
nonlinear optimization

01:20:02.800 --> 01:20:06.240
problem, such that it gives
us a solution in the end.

01:20:06.240 --> 01:20:09.720
And so, I'm sure you will be
going on this roller coaster

01:20:09.720 --> 01:20:12.080
also through your psets.

01:20:12.080 --> 01:20:17.320
And this concludes the open
loop portion of our roadmap.

01:20:17.320 --> 01:20:20.480
And on Monday, we'll be moving
towards closed loop methods

01:20:20.480 --> 01:20:22.240
through dynamic programming.

01:20:22.240 --> 01:20:24.070
Thank you.

